  "worked_solution": "First, we find the mixed partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}({f}_{x}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(axy-{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =ax-0 \\\\  & =ax \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}({f}_{y}(x,y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(y-5b{x}^{2}-7) \\\\  & =0-10bx-0 \\\\  & =-10bx\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal. Hence, we have the identity\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ax & =-10bx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients of $x,$ we get $b=-\\frac{a}{10}.$ \n\nNow, since ${f}_{x}(1,2)=21$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(1,2) & =19 \\\\ a\\cdot 2\\cdot 1-{1}^{2} & =19 \\\\ 2a & =20 \\\\ a & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nb=-\\frac{a}{10}=-\\frac{10}{10}=-1.\n$$",
